/**
 * @mainpage TowerDefense
 *
 * @section intro_sec Overview
 *
 * TowerDefense is a COMP345 assignment implementing the classic
 * tower-defense genre: critters walk a fixed path across a grid, the
 * player places and upgrades towers alongside that path to destroy them
 * before they reach the exit, and losing critters at the exit costs the
 * player coins. This page summarizes the genre rules the project is
 * based on and the software design used to implement them; browse the
 * *Classes* and *Files* tabs for the full API reference.
 *
 * @section rules_sec Game Rules
 *
 * Tower defense is a well-established strategy game subgenre where the
 * player defends a path or area from waves of enemies by building
 * stationary, upgradeable defenses rather than controlling units
 * directly (see the Tower Defense genre overview at
 * https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tower_defense). This project implements
 * the genre's core rules as follows:
 *
 * - **Map and path.** The map is a grid with one entry cell and one exit
 *   cell; a connected path between them is generated and validated with
 *   a breadth-first search (see Game::Map::isValid()). Critters always
 *   follow this path; towers may only be built on the remaining
 *   "scenery" cells beside it. A map editor lets the user create custom
 *   maps by choosing the grid dimensions (for example, 8x8, 9x12),
 *   designating the entry and exit cells, and assigning cells to the path
 *   or scenery.
 * - **Critters.** Each wave spawns critters (Game::WalkerCritter,
 *   Game::SpeedyCritter, Game::BossCritter) that walk from entry to exit
 *   at their own speed and with their own hit points. A critter that
 *   reaches the exit without being killed reduces the player's HP by 1.
 *   While it remains on the exit cell, it also steals coins continuously:
 *   each game tick deducts a fraction based on its strength and the time
 *   elapsed (Game::Critter::reachedEnd,
 *   App::CritterBillingObserver).
 * - **Towers.** The player buys towers (Game::DirectDammageTower,
 *   Game::AoeTower, Game::SlowTower) with coins and places them on
 *   scenery cells. A tower automatically targets and fires at critters
 *   within its range (Game::Tower::Update()); when several critters are
 *   in range, its Game::TargetingStrategy decides which one to shoot
 *   (nearest to the tower, nearest to the exit, strongest or weakest).
 *   The player selects a tower and presses the TARGET button to cycle
 *   its strategy; the current one is shown under the button and on the
 *   tower's inspection panel.
 *   A tower can be upgraded through further levels for more range,
 *   power and rate of fire, or sold back for a partial refund.
 * - **Economy and progression.** The player starts with a fixed coin
 *   balance (App::Player, `STARTING_COINS`). Killing a critter rewards
 *   coins; later waves field more, stronger and faster critters, which
 *   is the central escalating-difficulty loop common to the genre.
 * - **Winning and losing.** The player wins by surviving all five waves
 *   without their HP reaching 0 (App::WaveManager, `WIN_WAVE_COUNT`).
 *   The game is lost when the player's HP reaches 0.
 *
 * This genre has no single official rulebook; the specific numbers used
 * here (starting coins, pool sizes, wave composition, tower costs and
 * stats) are this project's own balancing choices layered on top of the
 * genre conventions cited above, and are documented on the relevant
 * classes (Game::Tower, App::WaveManager, Game::Critter and their
 * subclasses).
 *
 * @section design_sec Design
 *
 * The codebase is split into four namespaces with a one-way dependency
 * chain (App depends on Game and Renderer, which both depend on
 * Math):
 *
 * - **Math** - generic vector/color primitives (Math::Vec3, Math::Vec4,
 *   Math::Color) used wherever positions, sizes or colors are passed
 *   around.
 * - **Renderer** - a thin SDL2 wrapper with no gameplay knowledge: it
 *   owns the window and renderer (Renderer::Window, Renderer::Renderer),
 *   draws textures/sprite-sheets and solid shapes (Renderer::Texture,
 *   Renderer::Rectangle), and renders the map grid and UI buttons
 *   (Renderer::RendererMap, Renderer::MapGenerator, Renderer::Button).
 *   Renderer::GameRenderer is the facade Game code talks to instead of
 *   touching these pieces directly.
 * - **Game** - all gameplay rules and state, independent of how they are
 *   drawn or how input is captured.
 * - **App** - the executable layer: App::Application owns the window,
 *   the input manager and the game manager, and drives the main loop.
 *
 * Within Game, a few recurring patterns implement the rules above:
 *
 * - **Polymorphic critters/towers.** Game::Critter and Game::Tower are
 *   abstract base classes; each concrete critter/tower type only
 *   overrides the handful of virtual methods that actually differ
 *   between types (e.g. Game::Critter::setLevel(), Game::Tower::shoot()),
 *   so adding a new critter or tower type means implementing one small
 *   class rather than touching shared logic. Game::CritterFactory
 *   centralizes construction so callers don't need to know the concrete
 *   type.
 * - **Strategy (tower targeting).** A tower works in two steps: it first
 *   collects the living critters within range (identical for every
 *   tower, done by Game::Tower::selectTarget()), then asks its
 *   Game::TargetingStrategy to choose one of them. Each rule is a small
 *   stateless subclass (Game::NearestToTowerStrategy,
 *   Game::NearestToExitStrategy, Game::StrongestStrategy,
 *   Game::WeakestStrategy) built by Game::makeTargetingStrategy(), and a
 *   tower can swap its strategy at any time with
 *   Game::Tower::setTargetingStrategy() or
 *   Game::Tower::cycleTargetingStrategy() without any other tower code
 *   changing. Each tower type starts with its own default (direct:
 *   weakest, slow: nearest to exit, AoE: strongest). Ties go to the first
 *   critter in the candidate list, and "nearest to exit" uses
 *   Game::Critter::getDistanceToExit(), the distance still left to walk
 *   along the path. Changing the strategy is not an upgrade, so it does
 *   not notify the tower's observers.
 *
 *   The four rules, in the order the TARGET button cycles them:
 *   - **Near Tower** - the critter closest to the tower (grid distance,
 *     the same metric as the range check).
 *   - **Near Exit** - the critter with the least path left to walk.
 *   - **Strongest** - the critter with the most hit points left.
 *   - **Weakest** - the critter with the fewest hit points left.
 *
 *   "Strongest" and "weakest" use the critter's *current* hit points, the
 *   usual tower defense convention (for example the Strong / First / Last
 *   / Close targeting options in Bloons TD). The class diagram is in
 *   `Docs/UMLDiagrams/Ass3/Tower-Strategy.puml`.
 * - **Object pooling.** Critters and bullets are pre-allocated once by
 *   App::GameManager (`SpawnWalker`/`SpawnSpeed`/`SpawnBoss`,
 *   `SpawnBullets`) and simply activated/deactivated over the game's
 *   lifetime (Game::Critter::setIsActive(), Game::Bullet::GetActive())
 *   instead of being allocated and freed every wave/shot.
 * - **State machine wave progression.** App::WaveManager drives a
 *   small waiting -> spawning -> playing state machine (App::WaveState)
 *   that paces critters into the map and detects when a wave has been
 *   cleared, decoupling "when to spawn what" from the critters
 *   themselves.
 * - **Callback-driven input.** App::InputManager exposes
 *   `Attach*Event()` registration methods instead of exposing raw SDL
 *   state; App::GameManager attaches its own handlers
 *   (`HandleMousePress`/`HandleMouseMove`/`HandleMouseRelease`) so input
 *   handling and gameplay logic stay decoupled.
 * - **Request/effect objects for combat.** Rather than a tower directly
 *   mutating a critter, Game::Tower::shoot() fills in a
 *   Game::BulletRequest (carrying damage, target and an effect code);
 *   App::GameManager turns that into a pooled Game::Bullet, which on
 *   impact applies its effect (plain damage, an area-of-effect
 *   Game::HitRequest, or a slow via Game::Critter::halfSpeed()) depending
 *   on the tower type that fired it.
 */
